// XFAIL: DF-232e a GENERIC raw-backed enum loses its declared tag values at
// monomorphization and silently emits ordinals instead
//
// Found Aug 17 by DF-232c's consumer sweep over `EnumVariant.raw_value`, and
// pre-existing — nothing about the const-expression grammar is involved, and
// the literal spelling below fails exactly as an expression one would.
//
// `codegen/generics.py` rebuilds each `EnumVariant` with only `name` and
// `associated_types`, dropping `raw_value`, and calls `_register_concrete_enum`
// without `raw_type`. The concrete instantiation therefore has no backing at
// all, so `codegen/core.py` falls back to `variant_tags[name] = i` — the
// ordinal. `E<Int>.B` with `case B = 9` comes back as 1.
//
// SILENT is what makes it serious: a raw backing exists to pin an ABI number,
// and this hands back a different number than the source states, with no
// diagnostic at any phase. A non-generic `enum E: UInt8` is unaffected, which
// is why the whole existing corpus passes.
//
// NEEDS A RULING, which is why it is filed rather than fixed. Two defensible
// answers: carry `raw_value`/`raw_type` through the monomorphizer (they are
// instantiation-independent, so this looks correct and small), or REFUSE a raw
// backing on a generic enum outright, since design 145 never took the question
// of what a per-instantiation ABI number would mean. The second is the
// conservative reading of "declaring a backing says the numbers are ABI".
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// b=9
// a=7

enum Tagged<T>: UInt8 {
    case A = 7,
    case B = 9,
}

func main() {
    let b: Tagged<Int> = Tagged<Int>.B
    print("b={}", b as UInt8)
    let a: Tagged<Int> = Tagged<Int>.A
    print("a={}", a as UInt8)
}
